+### Autoheal
+
+Docker marks a container *unhealthy* when its `HEALTHCHECK` keeps
+failing, but does nothing about it (a restart policy only acts when the
+container exits). A container's page has an **Autoheal** panel
+(`action.power` to change it): with it on,
+debcontrol restarts the container when a monitoring sample finds it
+unhealthy
+([`app/services/autoheal.py`](https://github.com/sparkycz1/debcontrol/blob/main/app/services/autoheal.py)).
+
+- **At most N restarts an hour** (1-10, 3 by default), and at least 3
+  minutes between two, so a container that keeps failing isn't restarted
+  in a loop. Once the limit is reached debcontrol **gives up** until the
+  hour has passed, and says so - on the page, in the audit log and in a
+  notification.
+- Only a running container that is unhealthy right now; *starting*
+  (still in its start period) is left alone. For a container without a
+  health check the panel says there is nothing to act on, and how to add
+  one.
+- debcontrol's own containers are never restarted this way.
+- The restart is the same job as the *Restart* button. Each restart or
+  giving up is audited as `machine.docker.container.autoheal` (actor
+  *autoheal (automatic)*, `result` `restarted` or `gave_up`) and fires
+  the `docker.container.autoheal` event; changing the setting is
+  `machine.docker.container.autoheal_settings`.
+
+Off by default for every container.
+
