+### Endpoint checks for container web links
+
+With **Settings → Checks & retention → Endpoint checks for container web
+links** on (off by default), every link above of a running container
+gets an HTTP [endpoint check](Checks-and-Status-Page), kept in step with
+each monitoring sample
+([`app/services/docker_endpoint_checks.py`](https://github.com/sparkycz1/debcontrol/blob/main/app/services/docker_endpoint_checks.py)):
+
+- a link that appears gets a check (every 5 minutes); one that
+  disappears - the container stopped or was removed, or no longer offers
+  it - loses its check, history included;
+- `debcontrol.check=false` on a container leaves it out;
+  `debcontrol.check-path=/health` checks that path instead of the link's;
+- the checks carry an *auto* badge on the Checks page. You may change one
+  (interval, expected text, notifications) - only whether it exists
+  follows the container. Turning the setting off removes them all;
+- at most 40 per machine.
+
+Each check runs **from the container's machine** (*Run from*, see
+[Running a check from a managed machine](Checks-and-Status-Page#running-a-check-from-a-managed-machine)),
+never from the debcontrol server: the addresses come from labels anyone
+with access to that machine can write, so the server doesn't send
+requests to them. HTTPS on a published port (usually a self-signed
+certificate) is checked without certificate verification, a host name
+behind a proxy with it.
+
 **Image links.** Next to a newer image version: *Registry* (the image's
 page on Docker Hub, GHCR or Quay) and, from the image's own
 `org.opencontainers.image.source` label, *Source* and *Release notes*
@@ -410,6 +475,36 @@ prune commands as a job and reports the space reclaimed
